        "thought_text": "I apologize for the oversight. The error in the previous solution was due to a greedy approach that didn't consider the impact of removing edges on the overall connectivity of the graph. A better approach would be to use a more sophisticated algorithm that considers the global structure of the graph when deciding which edges to keep. Instead of prioritizing edges based solely on weight, we should consider the impact of removing an edge on the overall connectivity of the graph. This can be done by using a minimum spanning tree (MST) algorithm and then evaluating the contribution of each edge to the MST. Edges that, when removed, disconnect the MST or significantly reduce its total weight, should be considered for removal. This approach ensures that we maintain a connected graph while maximizing the number of good vertices.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-1226831817334304440",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_8_8f69ae_0",
